SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Annual Report of the Inter-American Commission on Human Rights 2022: Mexico","publisher":"Inter-American Commission on Human Rights","publication_date":"2023-03-07","url":"https://www.oas.org/en/iachr/docs/annual/2022/Chapters/14-IA2022_Cap_5_MX_EN.pdf","claim":"Mexico's 2022 reforms transferred National Guard control to Sedena, extended armed-forces participation in public security to 2028, and raised concerns about civilian oversight and militarization.","locator":"PDF pp. 1005-1008, paragraphs 9-17; lines 130-185 and 227-238","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-3B-01","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.oas.org/en/iachr/docs/annual/2022/Chapters/14-IA2022_Cap_5_MX_EN.pdf|pp.1005-1008|National Guard transfer and militarization","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["3B"],"methodology_ids":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.5","3B.6","3B.10"],"source_type":"regional human-rights monitoring report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent IACHR follow-up based on state submissions, civil-society evidence, hearings, and public records.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Evaluates information submitted for 2022 and records September-October 2022 reforms.","ruler_attribution":"Strong federal policy attribution, with Congress and courts as material constraints; not proof of personal responsibility for every abuse.","contrary_evidence":["The Mexican State characterized the National Guard as professional and rights-respecting, and reported that its purpose was to protect life, liberty, integrity, and property."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.5","3B.6","3B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Annual Report of the Inter-American Commission on Human Rights 2022: Mexico","publisher":"Inter-American Commission on Human Rights","publication_date":"2023-03-07","url":"https://www.oas.org/en/iachr/docs/annual/2022/Chapters/14-IA2022_Cap_5_MX_EN.pdf","claim":"The IACHR reported that National Guard use-of-force reports were not publicly available and that CNDH complaints from January-April 2022 included allegations of arbitrary use of force.","locator":"PDF pp. 1009-1010, paragraphs 25-28; lines 320-337","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-3B-02","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.oas.org/en/iachr/docs/annual/2022/Chapters/14-IA2022_Cap_5_MX_EN.pdf|pp.1009-1010|National Guard reporting opacity and arbitrary force complaints","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["3B"],"methodology_ids":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.9"],"source_type":"regional human-rights monitoring report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"IACHR assessment with statutory requirements and cited CNDH complaint material.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers January-April 2022 complaints and 2022 reporting obligations.","ruler_attribution":"Institutional federal-security attribution; complaints are not themselves findings against AMLO or individual officers.","contrary_evidence":["IACHR found formal compliance with the existence of a national use-of-force law while continuing to monitor implementation."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Annual Report of the Inter-American Commission on Human Rights 2022: Mexico","publisher":"Inter-American Commission on Human Rights","publication_date":"2023-03-07","url":"https://www.oas.org/en/iachr/docs/annual/2022/Chapters/14-IA2022_Cap_5_MX_EN.pdf","claim":"The State reported extensive National Guard training in human rights, use of force, gender, non-discrimination, and humanitarian principles, but the IACHR kept compliance pending because effects on civilian policing were not demonstrated.","locator":"PDF pp. 1008-1009, paragraphs 18-21; lines 239-284","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-3B-03","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.oas.org/en/iachr/docs/annual/2022/Chapters/14-IA2022_Cap_5_MX_EN.pdf|pp.1008-1009|National Guard training and pending compliance","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["3B"],"methodology_ids":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.7","3B.9"],"source_type":"regional human-rights monitoring report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The report preserves both government training claims and the IACHR's qualification.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Training figures include programs through December 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Federal institutional responsibility for policy and training; operational outcomes remain shared.","contrary_evidence":["The IACHR commended training progress even while finding the broader recommendation pending."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.7","3B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Annual Report of the Inter-American Commission on Human Rights 2022: Mexico","publisher":"Inter-American Commission on Human Rights","publication_date":"2023-03-07","url":"https://www.oas.org/en/iachr/docs/annual/2022/Chapters/14-IA2022_Cap_5_MX_EN.pdf","claim":"The IACHR recorded the case of four-year-old Heidi Mariana, reportedly shot and killed by the Army in Tamaulipas on 31 August 2022, and expressed concern about premature official statements regarding security-force legality.","locator":"PDF pp. 1010-1011, paragraphs 31-34; lines 370-388","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-3B-04","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.oas.org/en/iachr/docs/annual/2022/Chapters/14-IA2022_Cap_5_MX_EN.pdf|pp.1010-1011|Heidi Mariana and premature official statements","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["3B"],"methodology_ids":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.7","3B.9"],"source_type":"regional human-rights monitoring report","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"IACHR preserves civil-society reporting and expressly characterizes its own concern, while the underlying killing remained contested.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Incident dated 31 August 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Federal military conduct and official communication are institutionally attributable; no personal order by AMLO is established.","contrary_evidence":["The report records that the family disputed the official confrontation account; final judicial findings were not established in this source."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.7","3B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: Militarization of public security will lead to more human rights violations and perpetuate impunity","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-09","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/09/mexico-militarizing-public-security-human-rights-violations-impunity/","claim":"Amnesty reported more than 1,100 CNDH complaints against the National Guard between 2020 and 2022 involving alleged disappearances, arbitrary detention, unlawful killings, and torture, and said AMLO proposed further militarization on 31 August 2022.","locator":"Lines 70-86","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-3B-05","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/09/mexico-militarizing-public-security-human-rights-violations-impunity/|70-86|National Guard complaints and militarization proposal","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["3B"],"methodology_ids":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.5","3B.6","3B.10"],"source_type":"international human-rights analysis","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Amnesty assessment citing CNDH records and a UN treaty-body recommendation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly concerns the 2022 proposal and complaints through 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Strong attribution for the reform proposal and national security strategy; complaints remain allegations unless separately adjudicated.","contrary_evidence":["The administration presented military involvement as necessary to confront persistent criminal violence and emphasized training and discipline."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.5","3B.6","3B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: Extending Military Policing Threatens Rights","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-08-26","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/08/26/mexico-extending-military-policing-threatens-rights","claim":"Human Rights Watch warned that extending military policing would preserve powers to detain and interrogate civilians, detain migrants, track phones, and conduct law enforcement despite documented allegations of torture, disappearances, executions, and weak accountability.","locator":"Lines 610-620","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-3B-06","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/08/26/mexico-extending-military-policing-threatens-rights|610-620|military policing powers and alleged abuses","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["3B"],"methodology_ids":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.5","3B.6","3B.7"],"source_type":"international human-rights analysis","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous HRW assessment with specific powers, abuse patterns, and accountability concerns.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published during the August 2022 reform debate; some cited abuses are longitudinal context.","ruler_attribution":"Policy direction is attributable to AMLO's administration; historical military abuses are not automatically personal presidential conduct.","contrary_evidence":["The government and supporters argued that military discipline and territorial reach were needed to address criminal violence."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.5","3B.6","3B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: Army used Pegasus spyware against journalists and activists","publisher":"ARTICLE 19, R3D, SocialTIC, and Citizen Lab","publication_date":"2022-10-04","url":"https://www.article19.org/resources/mexico-army-spyware-journalists-activists/","claim":"The investigation reported that Pegasus spyware was used against journalists and a human-rights defender during the current administration, identified Army contracts through an intermediary, and said targets were investigating armed-forces abuses.","locator":"Lines 54-81","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-3B-07","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.article19.org/resources/mexico-army-spyware-journalists-activists/|54-81|Army Pegasus surveillance of journalists and defender","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["3B"],"methodology_ids":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.5","3B.8"],"source_type":"civil-society digital-forensics investigation","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Named organizations used forensic analysis and identified specific targets, contracts, and complaint action; the claim remains an investigative allegation pending adjudication.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published 4 October 2022; surveillance occurred in 2019-2021 but was disclosed during the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Strong institutional attribution to the Army and administration-era security apparatus; no proof that AMLO personally ordered targeting.","contrary_evidence":["The government denied or disputed current Pegasus use, and the investigation called for independent inquiry rather than treating the claims as judicial findings."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.5","3B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: Voices Against Indifference","publisher":"ARTICLE 19","publication_date":"2023-03-28","url":"https://www.article19.org/resources/mexico-voices-against-indifference/","claim":"ARTICLE 19 recorded 696 attacks against the press in 2022, 12 journalist murders, 181 intimidation or harassment cases, and at least 176 stigmatizing comments by the executive branch toward media, journalists, and civil society.","locator":"Lines 54-77","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-3B-08","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.article19.org/resources/mexico-voices-against-indifference/|54-77|2022 press attacks and executive stigmatization","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["3B"],"methodology_ids":["3B.3","3B.7","3B.8","3B.9"],"source_type":"press-freedom monitoring report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Annual organization report with defined 2022 counts and actor classifications.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective report specifically covering 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Executive comments are directly attributable at branch level; attacks and murders involve mixed state, criminal, and local actors.","contrary_evidence":["The report does not establish that every attack was caused by federal policy or that every presidential comment incited physical violence."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.7","3B.8","3B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"En caso Ayotzinapa, hay relación entre criminales y autoridades: GIEI","publisher":"Milenio","publication_date":"2022-09-29","url":"https://www.milenio.com/policia/caso-ayotzinapa-relacion-criminales-autoridades-giei","claim":"Reporting on the GIEI's third Ayotzinapa report stated that communications indicated close relationships between Guerreros Unidos and personnel from the Navy, forensic services, mayors, and police.","locator":"Headline, lines 166-183","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-3B-09","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.milenio.com/policia/caso-ayotzinapa-relacion-criminales-autoridades-giei|166-183|GIEI reported criminal-authority relationships in Ayotzinapa","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["3B"],"methodology_ids":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.9"],"source_type":"independent-expert report coverage","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous reporting of GIEI findings, but the underlying GIEI report should remain the preferred primary record.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published 29 September 2022 and concerns the administration's active investigation of the case.","ruler_attribution":"Evidence concerns state institutions and the administration's accountability challenge, not a personal order by AMLO or proof against every named official.","contrary_evidence":["The government created and supported the truth commission and continued searches, arrests, and document review; criminal-authority relationships remained allegations requiring judicial resolution."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Annual Report of the Inter-American Commission on Human Rights 2022: Mexico","publisher":"Inter-American Commission on Human Rights","publication_date":"2023-03-07","url":"https://www.oas.org/en/iachr/docs/annual/2022/Chapters/14-IA2022_Cap_5_MX_EN.pdf","claim":"The IACHR reported that Mexican authorities lacked updated information on independent oversight of lethal-force operations and that implementation of accountability mechanisms remained partial or pending.","locator":"PDF pp. 1012-1013 and p. 1041, paragraphs 40 and 167-172; lines 460-466 and 1681-1724","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-3B-10","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.oas.org/en/iachr/docs/annual/2022/Chapters/14-IA2022_Cap_5_MX_EN.pdf|pp.1012-1013,1041|incomplete independent lethal-force oversight","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["3B"],"methodology_ids":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.5","3B.8","3B.9"],"source_type":"regional human-rights monitoring report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"IACHR evaluates state submissions and civil-society evidence against prior court-ordered and treaty standards.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The report assesses implementation information for 2022, with historical compliance context.","ruler_attribution":"Institutional and policy-level attribution to the federal state; oversight gaps are not alone proof of presidential intent.","contrary_evidence":["Mexico reported a 2021 accountability protocol and statutory reporting requirements, although IACHR found insufficient implementation evidence."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.5","3B.8","3B.9"]}

