Documents discovered: approximately 30. Opened or attempted: 18; 12 underlying pages, reports, or PDFs were accessible and inspected. Accepted: 10 units. Rejected: 4 candidates for snippet-only, weak attribution, or inadequate period fit. Access-blocked: 6 government or archive endpoints.

New evidence: 10 units. Reused evidence: prior Ayotzinapa, IACHR, Amnesty, and military-governance records were reused for routing and cross-chapter context but not counted as new documents.

Distinct organizations: IACHR, Amnesty International, Human Rights Watch, ARTICLE 19, R3D, SocialTIC, Citizen Lab, and Milenio. Source types: regional monitoring report, NGO human-rights analysis, digital-forensics investigation, press-freedom report, and independent-expert reporting.

Lens coverage:

- 3B.1: IACHR, Amnesty, HRW, and Ayotzinapa evidence address disappearances, unlawful killing, torture, and arbitrary force.
- 3B.2: Military/National Guard complaints, alleged abuses, surveillance, and criminal-authority links are documented.
- 3B.3: ARTICLE 19 documents executive stigmatization; direct evidence of personal incitement to physical violence remains limited.
- 3B.4: Training, use-of-force law, truth-commission activity, and incomplete independent oversight are documented.
- 3B.5: National Guard militarization and Pegasus surveillance provide substantial evidence; legality and personal authorization remain partly unresolved.
- 3B.6: Persistent criminal violence and military expansion are documented; causal effects on public fear require more localized evidence.
- 3B.7: Child killing, journalist attacks, migrants, and vulnerable groups appear in the record, but women, Indigenous communities, and displacement require targeted supplementation.
- 3B.8: Press intimidation, stigmatization, surveillance, and chilling effects are documented.
- 3B.9: Ayotzinapa investigation and use-of-force cases provide crisis-response evidence; direct 2022 protest flare-up casework is sparse.
- 3B.10: The record shows continued insecurity and expanded militarization alongside investigative and training initiatives; a clean inherited-versus-2022 comparison remains unresolved.

Another Chapter 3B search wave would have material value for targeted case files involving women, children, migrants, Indigenous communities, protest policing, prison conditions, and 2022 judicial outcomes for military or National Guard abuses. Broad additional searching would mainly repeat the already well-supported militarization and press-freedom findings.
